      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "Ogilvy on Advertising in the Digital Age (Miles Young, 2017)", section: "Chapter 12 · The Three Battlegrounds · The Joy of Mobility; Old Mobile / New Mobile · PDF pp. 298–304", who: "Miles Young" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "What’s interesting is discussing what mobile enables, not what mobile is.", page: "298" },
        { text: "Bite-sized can bite back.", page: "301" }
      ],
      idea: "Young chose the heading carefully: the interesting question is what mobile *enables*, not what it *is*. In 2000, speaking at a mobile-technology conference in London, he argued the phone would be a **sales promotion** medium, not an advertising one. He was partly right: because the phone sits so close to the **point of decision**, it's especially good at steering people to offers and deals in a specific place. He still holds that it isn't an advertising medium of much significance, however often people try to use it as one. Mobility is a whole new lifestyle, and the premier platform of the digital age.\n\n**An engagement ecosystem.** His former colleague Martin Lange noted that phone networks have become utilities, spreading worldwide faster than clean water, electricity or sanitation. Phones are becoming boxes of sensors, and Apple (in the West) and WeChat (in China) stitched software, distribution, billing and the store into one seamless whole. Calvin Carter founded Bottle Rocket (now Ogilvy's mobile arm in Dallas) the day after Apple opened the iPhone to outside apps: he sketched five apps that night, four flopped, and the one for NPR was a runaway hit.\n\n**Two examples in these pages.** Nestlé's **Tummyfish** turns kids' water-drinking into a virtual pet that thrives or sulks with their daily choices. **Burger King**'s 15-second TV spot ended with a question to Google's voice assistant about what's in the Whopper, setting off thousands of phones and speakers in living rooms.\n\n**Bite-sized can bite back.** An app is only ever a bite-sized brand experience, and with millions of them, natural selection is brutal. Letting a brand onto your phone is a small but significant commitment; if the app fails, the experience is worse than none. Done right, mobile is disruptive: Carter calls it “Bricks and Mobile.” People pick up their phones 150–200 times a day, and nearly two-thirds of Americans keep theirs close overnight. His footwear-retail map puts the leaders (Amazon, Starbucks) in the corner that uses mobile for a holistic experience: getting people to the store, motivating them there, satisfying them after purchase and serving them, not just pushing coupons.\n\n**Old mobile vs new mobile.** Old: a shiny toy with no measurable impact; just another channel; old digital metrics; “every company needs an app”; context ignored. New: mobile-first (even mobile-only) engagement platforms; mobile as a service touchpoint offering utility or entertainment; mobile-specific metrics; focused investment matched to customer need; self-learning contextual systems like Google Now.\n\n**Why it's hard.** Less time per page, multitasking, app-switching, harder production, and the “airplane mode” problem: apps must keep working when the connection drops. Reformatting content to fit a small screen is only a small part of optimizing for mobile.",
