      source: [{ doc: "Ogilvy on Advertising in the Digital Age (Miles Young, 2017)", section: "Chapter 12 · The Three Battlegrounds · The Joy of Mobility: local intent, Lo-Fi to Hi-Fi, four passports to scale, the future; Five Mobile UX Mistakes box · PDF pp. 304–306, 308–309", who: "Miles Young" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Think of space and time before simply reformatting any old available content.", page: "304" },
        { text: "I have to tell you: the graveyard of small-scale apps is over-subscribed.", page: "305" }
      ],
      idea: "**Localize.** A Google and Nielsen study found **40% of mobile searches have local intent**, and the share is rising. In the developing world the effect is bigger. A service called **Tone** gives Indonesian fishermen a kit with a phone, learning materials and subsidized data; with GPS, weather and fish maps they learn more and catch more, so over time the service should pay for itself for carriers and users alike. Mobile-first content should be localized and useful, built from whatever data points are available: think about space and time first, then match content to the local context.\n\n**Lo-Fi first.** Calvin Carter of Bottle Rocket is “a techie who likes pencils.” Start with a pencil and a brutally clear view of what a small screen allows: **Lo-Fi techniques to reach Hi-Fi solutions**. Three reasons: (1) software tools start to shape what you make, while hand and paper have no rules; (2) you won't get it right first time, and cheap sketches make weak ideas easy to drop; (3) you can work in public: Bottle Rocket sketches on the walls to collaborate better. Bottle Rocket's **NPR One** app shows the payoff: local stations air shows at different times (or not at all), so the app gives on-demand listening to NPR programming wherever you are.\n\n**The four passports to scale.** The graveyard of small apps is crowded, so design with at least one of these:\n1. **Frictionless convenience**: make things more automatic, seamless and faster, with no pushy upsell. *SPG Keyless* lets Starwood hotel guests skip check-in, check-out and the front desk.\n2. **Substantive gratification**: extend a physical product with a mobile experience. *Lego Mindstorms EV3*: design a robot on the phone, build it in bricks, then drive it with the phone.\n3. **Hyper-personalization**: phones are intimate companions, and we tell them a lot about ourselves.\n4. **Ultra-context**: phones know the time, the place, the weather and more, yet apart from a few (like Google Now) brands barely use it.\n\n**Five mobile UX mistakes** (his box): treating mobile as just another digital channel; not being geared for mobile's speed of change; not using mobile analytics to build personal relationships; not recognizing how personal mobile is; and missing the shift to decentralized micro-interactions that serve the user's moment and context.\n\n**The future.** A billion more people online by 2020, mostly through mobile, plus 25–50 billion connected devices. Networks can't yet carry the data and 5G is far off, so services must work on today's networks and be affordable. Google and Facebook connect underserved communities partly to build segments they can monetize. Young hopes carriers, brands and consumers will share connectivity's benefits instead of creating just another cheap way to interrupt people with ads.",
