      quotes: [
        { text: "What they show, conclusively, is that emotional connection is the glue that holds omni-channel together.", page: "316" },
        { text: "Sometimes, it can be quite small things which trigger the emotion.", page: "318" }
      ],
      idea: "**Emotion in a hard-nosed business.** Hardcore ecommerce leaves emotion out for understandable reasons: it's built to be an efficient marketplace. Young argues emotion belongs in continuous commerce anyway, and brings data. Ogilvy's partner **Motista** spent seven years studying more than a billion emotional data points from 1.2 million US customers of hundreds of retailers. The finding: emotional connection holds omni-channel together. As Motista's CEO Scott Magids puts it, we want to shop where we feel we belong with other people. The “what” of commerce is easy to define; the “why” is emotional: belonging, sometimes escape or indulgence, or reinforcing the family.\n\n**The money.** Three segments: satisfied customers who shop only in store; satisfied customers who shop across channels; and emotionally connected customers who shop across channels. In luxury retail, annual spend per customer goes from **$637** (in store) to **$1,157** (omni-channel), nearly double, and to **$1,640** for emotionally connected omni-channel customers; Young calls it a 257% increase in sales per customer (about 2.6 times the in-store figure). Discount retail, where emotional connection is lowest, shows the same pattern.\n\n**Patagonia: small things, big emotion.** “Patagoniacs” have a personal relationship with the brand and smell phoniness instantly. The authenticity lives in details:\n- photography on patagonia.com of athletes in the wild, mostly shot by the adventurers themselves and always credited;\n- **“Notes from the Field”** essays in every print catalogue, a small addition with a big payoff;\n- the **Tin Shed** e-zine, a 360-degree microsite inside the shed where founder Yvon Chouinard started his climbing-gear business, with clickable objects that open ambassadors' stories and the catalogue always one click away;\n- **values up front**: staff can spend a month a year volunteering and 1% of sales goes to environmental groups, so Young's team, designing the site, put this in the main navigation rather than burying it in About Us;\n- the **Footprint Chronicles**, a tour of each garment's environmental and social impact, bad parts included, and a willingness to hand over the whole landing page when an issue mattered;\n- **Common Threads** with eBay, encouraging people to buy used gear or sell gear they no longer use, so products last longer;\n- ads like **“Unravelling Amy”** that simply restate the brand's beliefs: love of the outdoors, high-performance gear, care for the planet.\n\nPatagonia even gives people reasons *not* to buy its products, and that has become a competitive advantage: customers make more informed choices, loyalty runs deep, and Young describes the result as a sustainable and highly profitable loop.",
